# Tailscale's remaining kernel gaps.
#
# NOT the whole netfilter stack -- that is already present and was misdiagnosed
# once. The built kernel already has:
#
#   CONFIG_NETFILTER=y
#   CONFIG_NETFILTER_XTABLES=m
#   CONFIG_IP_NF_IPTABLES=m
#   CONFIG_TUN=y
#
# and ip_tables.ko / x_tables.ko load fine. What failed was Debian's default
# iptables-NFT backend against a kernel with CONFIG_NF_TABLES unset:
#
#   iptables: Failed to initialize nft: Protocol not supported
#
# That is fixed in userspace, not here, and it is what actually unblocked
# tailscaled -- see the comment in device-config on /etc/default/tailscaled:
#
#   update-alternatives --set iptables  /usr/sbin/iptables-legacy
#   update-alternatives --set ip6tables /usr/sbin/ip6tables-legacy
#
# With that done, tailscaled runs in normal mode with a real tailscale0 and
# both plain ssh and Tailscale SSH work. The two options below remove the two
# health warnings that remain. Neither is required for Tailscale to function,
# so this fragment is a tidy-up to fold into the next flash, not a fix.

# NOT ENABLED: xt_connmark. It crashes this kernel. (2026-08-27)
#
# "Extension connmark revision 0 not supported, missing kernel module?" is
# tailscaled failing to restore connection marks in mangle/PREROUTING, and
# xt_connmark is the module it wants. Building and loading it DOES clear that
# warning -- and then the kernel starts corrupting the conntrack extension area
# under ordinary traffic:
#
#   pc : kfree+0x2c/0x6c
#   lr : nf_conntrack_free+0x30/0xcc [nf_conntrack]
#        nf_ct_destroy -> nf_conntrack_destroy -> skb_release_head_state
#        -> kfree_skb_reason -> nf_hook_slow -> ip_output -> udp_sendmsg
#   Comm: tailscaled
#
# The attribution is not a guess. 76 boots of persistent journal going back to
# 2026-08-23 contain ZERO of these. The first is 2026-08-26 20:22:49, minutes
# after xt_connmark was first modprobed -- on kernel #90, so it is the MODULE
# and not the #91 flash. 82 oopses followed across six boots, all with an
# identical signature and 81 of 82 in tailscaled. One boot took 48, after which
# panfrost wedged ("gpu sched timeout", then "AS_ACTIVE bit stuck"),
# sddm-helper crashed, and the compositor died with "detected fb_set_par error,
# error code: -16" -- i.e. this is what was taking the desktop down.
#
# So the three symbols below stay OFF and the health warning stays. A warning
# is a better outcome than a kernel that corrupts memory under normal use, and
# nothing depends on connmark -- Tailscale runs, routes and passes traffic
# without it, which is how it ran for the 76 boots before this.
#
# If it is ever wanted, the bug has to be found first: the fault is a bad
# pointer inside the conntrack extension block at free time, so nf_ct_ext
# handling is where to look, with slub_debug=FZPU to attribute the corruption.
# Written as explicit "is not set" lines rather than deleted, so that
# gemini-kbuild.sh's post-merge assertion checks them on every build. If a
# future fragment or a defconfig change ever turns connmark back on, the build
# fails loudly instead of quietly shipping the crash again.
# CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_CONNMARK is not set
# CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_CONNMARK is not set
# CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_CONNMARK is not set

# "router: error querying IP rules (does kernel have IP_MULTIPLE_TABLES?):
#  operation not supported"
# Policy routing. Absent entirely from the current .config.
#
# IP_ADVANCED_ROUTER is a `depends on`, not a `select`, so asking for
# IP_MULTIPLE_TABLES alone gets it silently dropped by olddefconfig -- which is
# exactly what happened on the first build of this fragment (build #91,
# 2026-08-26): "DROPPED: CONFIG_IP_MULTIPLE_TABLES=y", caught by
# gemini-kbuild.sh's post-merge assertion and not by anything else. The parent
# symbol is a pure menu-gate: on its own it changes no code.
CONFIG_IP_ADVANCED_ROUTER=y
CONFIG_IP_MULTIPLE_TABLES=y

# Not added deliberately: CONFIG_NF_TABLES. Adding it would let the NFT backend
# work and make the update-alternatives step unnecessary, which is tempting.
# It also pulls in a large subsystem on a device where the connectivity stack is
# a vendor blob and every kernel change costs a flash and a boot-loop risk. The
# legacy backend is measured working; revisit only if something else needs nft.
